        <div id="seasoning-truth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does a Firewood Rack Dry Wood? Only With Airflow, Sun and a Top Cover
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people buy a firewood rack expecting it to season their wood. It does not. A rack is a steel frame that lifts wood off the ground and keeps the stack upright. The drying is done by moving air, sunshine and time, and the most common way people stop that drying is by wrapping the whole stack in a tarp or a full-length cover.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Actually Seasons Firewood</h3>

          <p>
            Freshly split wood is full of water, and that water has to leave through the cut ends and split faces. It leaves fastest when air moves through the stack and sun warms it. A rack helps in two ways: it keeps the bottom row out of the damp soil that would wick water back in, and it holds the stack in a single, open row so wind can pass through it. Put that row in a sunny, breezy spot, split the wood before you stack it, and you have done most of the work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why a Fully Covered Tarp Backfires</h3>

          <p>
            A tarp pulled down over the sides traps the moisture that is trying to leave the wood. On a warm day the water evaporates, hits the cooler tarp, condenses and drips back onto the logs. The stack stays damp, mold grows on the bark, and wood that should have been ready by autumn still hisses in the stove. The fix is simple: cover the top only, and leave the sides open to the air. A top cover keeps rain and snow off the upper rows, which is where most water gets in, while the sides keep breathing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Rack Earns Its Price</h3>

          <p>
            A rack earns its money by keeping wood off the ground, stopping a pile from collapsing into a heap, and making it easy to see how much you have left. It also keeps the stack tidy enough to put where it dries best, rather than wherever the pile happened to land. Buy a rack for those reasons and pair it with a top-only cover, and it will help your wood season. Buy one expecting the steel to do the drying, and you will be disappointed.
          </p>
        </div>

