          </h2>

          <p>
            The right rack depends on how much wood you burn, where you can store it, and whether it lives in the yard or beside the stove. Four types cover nearly every home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor Tube Racks for a Season of Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Long steel racks, usually 4 or 8 feet, are what most wood-burning homes need for their main supply. The Woodhaven, Pleasant Hearth and Amagabeli are this type. Look at depth as closely as length: a 13 to 14 inch rack suits standard 16-inch logs, while a 24-inch-deep rack like the Woodhaven holds much more wood in the same footprint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bracket Kits for a Custom Length</h3>

          <p>
            Bracket kits such as the Shelter SLRA and Mr IRONSTONE give you the steel corners and let 2x4s set the length. They are the cheapest way to fill an awkward space or build a long run along a fence, at the cost of wooden rails that will weather and need replacing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hoops and Indoor Log Holders</h3>

          <p>
            Round hoops and small holders, like the Sunnydaze 48-inch and UniFlame 36-inch, hold a few days to a few weeks of wood near the door, on a deck or beside the hearth. They are staging racks, refilled from the main stack, not places to season green wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            Burn more than a cord a winter: an 8-foot rack, or two, in the sun. Burn on weekends or for a fire pit: a 4-foot rack or a hoop. Have an odd space or need more than 8 feet: a bracket kit. Want wood indoors: a small hoop well clear of the stove.
          </p>

