        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Firewood Rack Do You Need? Cords, Face Cords and the Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Firewood is sold by the cord, and racks are rated in fractions of one, but those ratings are often looser than they look. A little arithmetic tells you what a rack really holds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Full Cord Is 128 Cubic Feet</h3>

          <p>
            A full cord is a stack 4 feet high, 4 feet deep and 8 feet long, or 128 cubic feet of wood and air. That is a lot of wood: two 8-foot rows of 24-inch logs, or three rows of 16-inch logs. Many homes that heat mainly with a wood stove burn several cords a winter, while a fireplace used on weekends may go through a fraction of one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Face Cord Varies With Log Length</h3>

          <p>
            A face cord, sometimes called a rick or stove cord, is a stack 4 feet high and 8 feet long but only one log deep. Its volume depends on the log length, which is why the term varies from seller to seller. With common 16-inch logs, a face cord is about 43 cubic feet, roughly a third of a full cord. Always ask a firewood seller how long the logs are, and treat face cord prices with that in mind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check a Rack's Cord Claim Yourself</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply length by height by depth in feet. An 8-foot rack 4 feet tall and about 14 inches deep holds roughly 37 cubic feet, a little under a face cord of 16-inch logs with the ends overhanging. Several listings call that size a half cord, which is only true with 24-inch logs. The Woodhaven's half-cord rating holds up because the rack is built for 24-inch-deep stacks: 8 by 4 by 2 feet is 64 cubic feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Rack to Your Burning</h3>

          <p>
            Estimate what you burn in a winter, then plan storage for a little more, since next year's wood should be drying while you burn this year's. Two racks, one seasoning and one in use, is the simplest system for anyone who heats with wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="covers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Firewood Rack Covers: Why Top-Only Beats Full-Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cover keeps rain and snow off your wood, but how much of the stack it covers decides whether the wood keeps drying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Top Covers for Seasoning Wood</h3>

          <p>
            A top cover, or a short cover that hangs down only a foot or so, sheds water from the upper rows and leaves the sides open. This is the right choice for wood that is still drying. Woodhaven's cover is designed this way, to keep the top rows dry while the exposed sides keep seasoning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Full-Length Covers for Seasoned Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Full-length covers that wrap the whole rack, like the one in the Amagabeli kit, are best for wood that is already dry and simply needs protecting from wind-driven rain and snow near the house. Even then, vents matter. Open the front on dry days so moisture can escape, and close it before a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tarps and DIY Covers</h3>

          <p>
            A plain tarp works if you lay it over the top and weigh it down without pulling it down the sides. Sheet metal, old roofing panels or plywood laid on top also work well and do not flap in the wind. Avoid wrapping any plastic tightly around the stack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Put a Firewood Rack: Off the Ground, Away From the House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where you put the rack matters as much as which rack you buy. The best spot is sunny, breezy, level and not against your home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Wood Away From the House</h3>

          <p>
            A stack of wood is a home for termites, carpenter ants, mice and spiders. Pest-control and extension services commonly advise keeping the main woodpile well away from the house, often 20 feet or more, and never stacking it against siding or a foundation. Keep only a small supply near the door, and bring wood indoors as you burn it rather than storing it inside for weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Off the Ground</h3>

          <p>
            Wood sitting on soil wicks up moisture and gives insects an easy way in. Every rack here raises the bottom row, and a few inches of clearance makes a real difference. Put the rack on gravel, pavers or a concrete pad rather than grass or bare dirt, so the ground stays drier and weeds do not grow into the stack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Find Sun and Wind</h3>

          <p>
            Face the long side of the rack toward the prevailing wind if you can, and choose a spot that gets several hours of sun. A shaded corner behind the garage is convenient, but wood there dries slowly. Leave a few inches of space between the stack and any fence or wall so air can move behind it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">On Decks and Patios</h3>

          <p>
            A small rack or hoop on a deck is convenient, but keep it away from the house wall, sweep up bark and debris often, and do not let it trap water against deck boards. Keep fire pits and grills at a safe distance from any woodpile, following the fire pit maker's clearance guidance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seasoning-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long to Season Firewood on a Rack: 6 to 12 Months, Under 20%
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seasoned firewood burns hotter, cleaner and with less creosote in the chimney. Wet wood wastes heat boiling off water and smolders.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan on Six to Twelve Months</h3>

          <p>
            General guidance is that split firewood needs six to twelve months of drying, sometimes longer. Softwoods and thinly split wood dry faster; dense hardwoods such as oak can take a year or more. Splitting before stacking speeds things up considerably, which is where a good <a href='/best-splitting-maul/'>splitting maul</a> or a <a href='/best-log-splitter/'>log splitter</a> earns its place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Aim for Under 20 Percent Moisture</h3>

          <p>
            Wood is generally considered ready to burn below about 20 percent moisture content. The only reliable way to know is a moisture meter: split a piece and test the fresh inside face, not the weathered outside. Our <a href='/best-moisture-meter/'>moisture meter guide</a> covers which meters read wood accurately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs Your Wood Is Ready</h3>

          <p>
            Seasoned wood is lighter than it looks, has cracks radiating from the ends, sounds hollow when two pieces are knocked together, and its bark comes away easily. If it hisses or bubbles in the stove, it still holds too much water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Steel Gauge, Tube Size and Powder Coating: What Makes a Rack Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            A loaded 8-foot rack can carry well over a thousand pounds, sitting outside in rain and snow for years. A few specs tell you how long it will stay straight and rust-free.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tube Diameter and Wall Thickness</h3>

          <p>
            Tube racks are listed by diameter, often 30 to 38mm or about 1.25 inches. Diameter helps stiffness, but wall thickness matters as much: a wide tube with a thin wall can still bend. Amagabeli lists 1.2mm walls. When a listing gives no wall thickness, as Pleasant Hearth's does not, check the maker's spec sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steel Gauge</h3>

          <p>
            Gauge runs backward: the lower the number, the thicker the steel. Woodhaven lists 16-gauge steel for its frame, and Mr IRONSTONE lists 13-gauge (2.95mm) plate for its brackets, which is thick for a part that carries the stack's corners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Powder Coating and Rust</h3>

          <p>
            Nearly every outdoor rack is powder coated, and a good coat is what keeps it from rusting in a season. Scratches from logs are inevitable, so touch up bare spots with rust-resistant paint each year. Stainless hardware, as Woodhaven lists, avoids rusted bolts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Legs, Bracing and Capacity Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            More legs and cross braces keep a long rack from bowing in the middle and from settling on soft ground. Treat weight ratings as a ceiling, not a target, and stack evenly from end to end so one side does not carry the load.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Indoor Firewood Racks: Hearth Hoops and Log Holders
          </h2>

          <p>
            An indoor holder saves trips outside in winter, but it is not for storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bring In Only a Few Days of Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Keep indoor wood to a day or a few days of burning. Wood stored inside for weeks gives insects hiding in the bark time to warm up and wander out, and a large indoor stack is more fuel near the fire than you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Clearance From the Stove</h3>

          <p>
            Place any log holder outside the clearances your wood stove or fireplace manufacturer specifies for combustibles, and follow local codes. Firewood is a combustible, and a holder pushed right up against a hot stove is a real fire risk. Our <a href='/best-wood-stove/'>wood stove guide</a> covers clearances in more detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Floor</h3>

          <p>
            Bark, dirt and moisture fall off firewood. A hearth pad, a boot tray or felt pads under the holder's feet protect wood floors, and a quick shake outside before you bring logs in keeps most of the debris and bugs out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stacking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Stack Firewood on a Rack So It Stays Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            A well-stacked rack dries faster and does not tip over on someone reaching for a log.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Split First, Then Stack Loosely</h3>

          <p>
            Split wood before it goes on the rack and stack it in a single row with small gaps, so air can reach every piece. Tight, perfectly fitted stacks look good but dry more slowly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bark Side Up on the Top Row</h3>

          <p>
            Many burners stack the top row bark side up so it sheds rain like shingles, and the lower rows any way that fits. Let log ends overhang a narrow rack slightly; the exposed ends dry fastest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Level and Within the Frame</h3>

          <p>
            Start on level ground, fill from both ends toward the middle, and do not stack higher than the rack's uprights. A stack that leans is a stack that will fall, and a full rack is heavy enough to hurt a child or pet. Anchor or brace tall homemade racks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="firewood-workflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. The Full Firewood Setup: Cutting, Splitting, Storing and Burning
          </h2>

          <p>
            A rack is one link in a chain that runs from the log to the stove. If wood is not drying well, the cause is often earlier in the chain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cutting and Splitting</h3>

          <p>
            Rounds cut to length with a <a href='/best-chainsaw/'>chainsaw</a> dry very slowly until they are split, because bark seals moisture in. Split them as soon as you can with a <a href='/best-splitting-maul/'>splitting maul</a> for modest amounts or a <a href='/best-log-splitter/'>log splitter</a> for several cords a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storing and Testing</h3>

          <p>
            Stack the splits on a rack in the sun with a top cover, label or rotate racks by year, and test a few pieces with a <a href='/best-moisture-meter/'>moisture meter</a> before the burning season starts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Burning</h3>

          <p>
            Dry wood in a well-maintained <a href='/best-wood-stove/'>wood stove</a> gives more heat and less creosote. If you want the fireplace look without hauling wood at all, an <a href='/best-electric-fireplace/'>electric fireplace</a> is the low-effort alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wrapping the Whole Stack in a Tarp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tarp pulled down the sides traps moisture, so the wood stays damp and grows mold. Cover the top only and leave the sides open to the air.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking Wood Against the House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A woodpile against siding or a foundation invites termites, carpenter ants and rodents. Keep the main rack well away from the house and only a small supply near the door.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Cord Rating on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many 14-inch-deep racks are sold as half-cord racks, but that is only true with 24-inch logs. Multiply length, height and depth to see what a rack really holds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking Unsplit Rounds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bark seals moisture in, so whole rounds can sit for years and stay wet. Split wood before it goes on the rack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Rack in the Shade on Bare Soil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A damp, shaded spot on grass or dirt keeps wood wet. Choose a sunny, breezy, level spot on gravel or pavers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

