        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best firewood rack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most wood-burning homes, the Woodhaven 8-Foot Raised Firewood Rack is the best firewood rack. Woodhaven lists 16-gauge U.S. steel, a capacity of 1/2 cord of 24-inch logs and a cover designed to keep the top rows dry while the sides breathe, which is how wood actually seasons. The Pleasant Hearth 8-foot rack is a simpler, open alternative for less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a firewood rack be covered?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cover the top, not the whole stack. A top cover keeps rain and snow off the upper rows while the open sides let moisture escape so the wood keeps drying. A tarp or full cover wrapped around the sides traps moisture and can leave wood damp and moldy. Full covers suit wood that is already seasoned, and should be opened on dry days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far should a firewood rack be from the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common pest-control and extension advice is to keep the main woodpile well away from the house, often 20 feet or more, because stacked wood shelters termites, carpenter ants and rodents. Never stack wood against siding or a foundation. Keep only a small supply near the door and bring wood indoors only as you burn it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much wood does an 8 foot firewood rack hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on depth. An 8-foot rack 4 feet tall and about 14 inches deep holds roughly a face cord of 16-inch logs, about a third of a full cord. A rack built for 24-inch-deep stacks, like the Woodhaven, holds 64 cubic feet, a half cord. A full cord is 4 by 4 by 8 feet, or 128 cubic feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does firewood need to season on a rack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                General guidance is six to twelve months after splitting, and longer for dense hardwoods such as oak. Wood is usually considered ready below about 20 percent moisture content. Split wood dries much faster than whole rounds, and a sunny, breezy spot with a top-only cover speeds it up. Check a freshly split face with a moisture meter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it OK to keep firewood indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep only a day or a few days of seasoned wood inside, in a holder placed outside the clearances your stove or fireplace maker specifies. Wood stored indoors for weeks can release insects as it warms, and wet wood adds moisture to the room. Store the main supply outdoors on a raised rack, away from the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a metal or wood firewood rack better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Powder-coated steel racks last longer outdoors and do not rot. Bracket kits that use 2x4s are cheaper and can be built to any length, but the wooden rails weather and need replacing over time. Pressure-treated lumber lasts longer for rails, but some brackets do not fit it, so check the listing, and never burn treated lumber.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

